---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import HomeEquityLoanCalculator from '@components/islands/HomeEquityLoanCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'home-equity-loan-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'What is a home equity loan?', answer: 'A home equity loan lets you borrow a lump sum against the equity you have built in your home, repaid at a fixed rate over a fixed term. Because it is secured by your property, it usually carries a lower rate than unsecured borrowing — but your home is the collateral.' },
  { question: 'How does a home equity loan work?', answer: 'You borrow a one-time lump sum based on your equity and the lender\'s loan-to-value limit, then repay it in equal monthly installments (principal plus interest) over the term, much like a second mortgage. The rate and payment are fixed for the life of the loan.' },
  { question: 'How much can I borrow against my home?', answer: 'Lenders cap your combined borrowing at a percentage of your home value — the combined loan-to-value (CLTV) limit, often 80–85%. Your maximum is that percentage of the home value minus what you still owe on your first mortgage.' },
  { question: 'What is a home equity loan versus a HELOC?', answer: 'A home equity loan gives you a lump sum at a fixed rate with fixed payments. A HELOC is a revolving line of credit you draw from as needed, usually at a variable rate. This calculator models the fixed-payment lump-sum loan.' },
  { question: 'Are home equity loans a good idea?', answer: 'They can be sensible for large, one-time costs — a renovation that adds value, or consolidating higher-interest debt — because the rate is lower than credit cards or personal loans. They are riskier for discretionary spending, since missing payments can put your home at risk.' },
  { question: 'Can you refinance a home equity loan?', answer: 'Yes. You can refinance a home equity loan into a new one, roll it into a HELOC, or fold it into a cash-out refinance of your first mortgage — usually to get a lower rate or a different term. Weigh any closing costs against the savings.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-03' }}
  intro="Estimate how much you can borrow against your home equity and the monthly payment on a home equity loan."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <HomeEquityLoanCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter your <strong>home value</strong> and current <strong>mortgage balance</strong> — the gap is your equity.</li>
      <li>Set the lender's <strong>maximum loan-to-value</strong> (the CLTV cap, often 80–85%).</li>
      <li>Enter the <strong>loan amount you want</strong>, an <strong>interest rate</strong> and a <strong>term</strong> in years.</li>
      <li>Read your equity, the most you could borrow within the cap, and the monthly payment — the tool flags a request that exceeds the limit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Equity versus what you can borrow</h2>
    <p>
      Your <strong>equity</strong> is the home's value minus what you still owe, but lenders rarely
      let you borrow all of it. They cap your <strong>combined loan-to-value</strong> (CLTV) — the
      total of your first mortgage plus the new loan — at a percentage of the home's value. Your
      borrowing room is that capped total minus the mortgage you already carry, which is usually
      well below your full equity.
    </p>

    <h2>The formulas</h2>
    <p>The calculator works out three figures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Equity = home value − mortgage balance</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Max borrow = home value × max LTV − mortgage balance</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Payment = L · r · (1 + r)<sup>n</sup> / ((1 + r)<sup>n</sup> − 1)</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In the payment formula, <strong>L</strong> is the loan amount you request, <strong>r</strong>
      is the monthly rate (annual rate ÷ 12), and <strong>n</strong> is the number of payments
      (years × 12) — the same fixed-rate amortization used for a mortgage.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Say your home is worth <strong>$400,000</strong> and you still owe <strong>$250,000</strong>.
      Your equity is <strong>$150,000</strong>, but you can't borrow all of it. At an
      <strong>85% CLTV</strong> limit, total debt against the home is capped at
      0.85 × $400,000 = $340,000. Subtract the $250,000 you already owe and about
      <strong>$90,000</strong> is available to borrow.
    </p>
    <p>
      Borrow that <strong>$90,000</strong> at <strong>8%</strong> over <strong>15 years</strong> and
      the payment is about <strong>$860.09 a month</strong>, with roughly <strong>$64,816</strong>
      of interest over the full term. Lenders also weigh your income, credit and debt-to-income
      ratio, so your approved amount may be lower than the equity-based maximum.
    </p>

    <h2>Home equity loan versus HELOC</h2>
    <p>
      Both borrow against equity, but they suit different needs. This tool models the fixed
      lump-sum loan:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Home equity loan</th><th>HELOC</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>How you receive it</td><td>One lump sum</td><td>Revolving line, draw as needed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interest rate</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Usually variable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payment</td><td>Fixed principal &amp; interest</td><td>Varies with balance and rate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Best for</td><td>A known, one-time cost</td><td>Staged or uncertain expenses</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A home equity loan is ideal when you know the exact cost — a set renovation or a debt
      consolidation — while a HELOC gives flexibility when the total is uncertain or spread over
      time.
    </p>

    <h2>What lenders check beyond equity</h2>
    <p>
      Available equity sets the ceiling, but approval depends on more. Lenders look at your
      <strong>credit score</strong>, your <strong>income</strong> and your
      <strong>debt-to-income ratio</strong> — how much of your monthly income already goes to debt.
      A home equity loan adds a second lien on your property, so they underwrite it much like a
      mortgage, and your final amount and rate reflect that whole picture.
    </p>

    <h2>Borrow carefully</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The loan is <strong>secured by your home</strong>, so missed payments can put the property at risk — a higher-stakes trade than unsecured borrowing.</li>
      <li>It is best suited to <strong>large, one-time costs</strong> that add value or replace higher-interest debt, not discretionary spending.</li>
      <li>Watch for <strong>closing costs and fees</strong>, which this calculator does not include — weigh them against the interest you would save.</li>
      <li>The estimate uses one fixed rate and does not model taxes, insurance or a lender's income and credit checks.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      To see the full principal-and-interest breakdown of the loan, use the
      <a href="/finance/amortization-calculator">amortization calculator</a>; to weigh it against
      your primary home loan, use the <a href="/finance/mortgage-calculator">mortgage calculator</a>.
      This is educational information for planning, not a loan offer or financial advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
